Done. Round d1_r5 work is complete: the change, the on-node evidence behind it, and the strategy record are all in place.

**What happened this round:** The reservation on the scoring node (a80n0) was alive all round — only masked by the known squeue-shim bug, which I worked around per d1_prime/d1_batchlane's records. That let me run the interleaved on-node A/B that three rounds of wallaby proxies couldn't settle: at L=60 B=512, the plain per-transform 256-bit `ymm1` kernel loop beats both batched pair kernels (0.045–0.046 us vs zmm2x2 0.052 / ymm2 0.053) at identical 3.3 GHz — it's ICX port contention (all 512-bit ops share p0+p5), not frequency licensing. I flipped the batched default accordingly, taking the one losing cell from 1.12x behind MKL to ~1.03x (0.044–0.046 vs 0.043–0.044, inside the noise floor), with B=1 m=1 winning at 0.041 vs MKL 0.054 and both chains untouched and far ahead (0.110 vs 0.270; 0.058 vs 0.227). Two ideas were killed cheaply by measurement before any rewrite: the 512-bit-license hypothesis for the chain (3.3 GHz sustained) and a shallower Newton map (no accuracy headroom). Full correctness sweep passes on the node: exec, strict m=2 gates, all four chain gates, edge batches, dense L=12/24/36 floor, bit-identical repeatability; all flag variants build `-Wall -Wextra` clean, and wallaby shows no SPR regression.

**One line:** Good–Thomas PFA-60 with the broadcast-fed signed-FMA ymm1 kernel now run per-transform at every batch size (256-bit mix beats 512-bit pair kernels on ICX ports); best wallaby 0.028 us/transform at B=1 and 0.032 us batched (B=512) — node: 0.041 and 0.044–0.046 vs MKL 0.054/0.043; rel L2 = 2.2e-16.
